## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a compact computer vision pipeline with practical on-device adaptation under tight latency constraints. It also probes understanding of data strategy and evaluation beyond single-object pose tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Lightweight detection with 2D bounding boxes
- Depth proxy estimation from object size
- Simple tracking with temporal consistency
- On-device few-shot adaptation (3-shot)
- Counting metrics and evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: detection + tracking update loop
for (const frame of video) {
  const detections = detector.predict(frame);
  const depthProxy = estimateDepth(detections, calibration);
  const tracked = tracker.update(detections, depthProxy);
  yield { boxes: tracked, count: countPackages(tracked) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a complete edge-CV pipeline: real-time monocular 3D pose with per-object velocity, occlusion robustness, and rapid on-device adaptation to unseen parts. Emphasizes practical data strategies and evaluation in an industrial setting.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time monocular 3D pose estimation
- Per-instance velocity prediction for planning
- On-device few-shot adaptation (5-shot)
- Occlusion handling and data fusion strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: forward pass with adaptation trigger
function forward(frame, exemplars){
  let features = backbone(frame);
  let detections = detector(features);
  let poses = poseHead(features, detections);
  let vel = velocityHead(features, detections, previous);
  if (exemplars) adapt(poses, exemplars);
  return {detections, poses, vel};
}
```

